<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aftasid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1022–<a href="/years/1094">1094</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Aftasids, Banu al-Aftas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aftasid dynasty ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>, the largest Muslim kingdom of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, from 1022 to 1094. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-aftas">Abd Allah ibn al-Aftas</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> descent, it held lands from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a>, and its court was known for poetry and learning. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> ended it in 1094, executing the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-al-mutawakkil">Umar al-Mutawakkil</a>, after he had ceded Lisbon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sintra">Sintra</a> and Santarém to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p></div>
